---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import { getAiSkillsSidebar } from '../../data/nav';
import { publishedSkills, skillsCliCommand } from '../../lib/skills';

const skills = await publishedSkills();
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="AI skills"
  section="ai-skills"
  description="Skills that apply the design system's own standards to AI-assisted work, and the standards that apply however the work was produced."
  pageTitle="AI skills"
  pageSubtitle="Reusable instructions that hold AI-assisted work to the same standards as any other."
  sidebarGroups={await getAiSkillsSidebar()}
  sidebarLabel="AI skills"
  onThisPage={[
    { label: 'Available skills', href: '#available-skills' },
    { label: 'Install', href: '#install' },
    { label: 'What a skill is', href: '#what-a-skill-is' },
    { label: 'Standards that apply either way', href: '#standards' },
  ]}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'AI skills' }]}
>
  <div class="govbb-prose">
    <h2 id="available-skills">Available skills</h2>

    {
      skills.length === 0 ? (
        <p>No skills are published yet.</p>
      ) : (
        <table class="govbb-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Skill</th>
              <th scope="col">Use it when</th>
              <th scope="col">Status</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {skills.map((skill) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  <a href={`/ai-skills/${skill.id}/`}>{skill.data.metadata.title}</a>
                </th>
                <td>{skill.data.description}</td>
                <td>
                  {skill.data.metadata.status === 'supported'
                    ? 'Supported'
                    : 'Experimental'}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      )
    }

    <p>
      <strong>Experimental</strong> means the skill works and has been used, but
      has not yet earned its keep across enough real services for us to promise
      it will not change. Read its page before relying on its output.
    </p>

    <h2 id="install">Install</h2>

    <p>
      If you are at GovTech, add the team marketplace once and install the
      <code>govbb</code> plugin. Skills then appear as
      <code>/govbb:&lt;name&gt;</code>:
    </p>

    <pre tabindex="0"><code>/plugin marketplace add govtech-bb/team-skills
/plugin install govbb@team-skills</code></pre>

    <p>
      The plugin is sourced from this design system's own repository, so a change
      here reaches you at your next start with nothing to update. If you added
      that marketplace before the <code>govbb</code> plugin existed, run
      <code>/plugin marketplace update team-skills</code> first — automatic
      updates cover new versions of a plugin, not the arrival of a new one.
    </p>

    <p>
      Outside GovTech, or to take a single skill into any repository, install it
      directly. No marketplace needed:
    </p>

    <pre tabindex="0"><code>{skills.length
      ? skillsCliCommand(skills[0].id)
      : 'npx skills add govtech-bb/govbb-design-system'}</code></pre>

    <p>
      Every skill also has a raw Markdown twin at its page URL plus
      <code>.md</code> — that is the file itself, so you can read it, review it,
      or hand it to a tool that has no installer.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-a-skill-is">What a skill is</h2>

    <p>
      A skill is a set of instructions an AI coding assistant loads when a task
      matches it. It is not a model, a service, or anything that runs on its own.
      It carries no data about your service anywhere.
    </p>

    <p>
      These skills exist because the same questions kept being answered
      differently: which component covers this, does this meet the accessibility
      standard, is this what the design system actually says. Writing the answer
      down once means every team gets the same one, and that answer can be
      reviewed and argued with like any other guidance.
    </p>

    <p>
      They deliberately hold no list of components, tokens or values. Those
      change, and a skill repeating a stale one gives confident wrong answers
      without anything failing to warn you. Instead each skill reads this site at
      the moment it runs, which is why guidance here and advice from a skill
      cannot drift apart.
    </p>

    <h2 id="standards">Standards that apply either way</h2>

    <p>
      A skill helps meet these; it does not replace them. AI-assisted work is held
      to the same design, accessibility, privacy and review standards as work
      produced any other way.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/documentation/using-the-design-system/">Follow the design-system standards</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Use published <a href="/components/">components</a> and
        <a href="/patterns/">patterns</a> instead of inventing alternatives.
      </li>
      <li>Review generated copy, code and interfaces before they reach users.</li>
      <li>
        Do not enter personal, confidential or security-sensitive information
        into an AI tool unless your organisation has approved that use.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      A skill's report is a starting point for a person, not a sign-off. Where a
      skill can only verify something partly, it is written to say so rather than
      imply it passed — if a report claims something you cannot reproduce, treat
      that as a defect in the skill and
      <a href="/support/">tell us</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</ArticleLayout>
